  meta="Gross vs net pay explained. Learn what's deducted from your salary and how to estimate your real take-home pay with a free calculator.",
  lead="Your salary and the amount that actually lands in your account are two different numbers. Knowing the gap is the first step to budgeting well.",
  body="<h2>Gross vs net</h2><p><strong>Gross</strong> is your salary before deductions. <strong>Net</strong> (take-home) is what's left after tax and other withholdings — the number your budget should be built on.</p><h2>What gets deducted</h2><ul><li>Income tax.</li><li>Social contributions (where applicable).</li><li>Any pension or benefit deductions.</li></ul><div class='callout'><p>&#128161; Always budget from your net pay. Building a plan around gross is the classic reason people run short before payday.</p></div><h2>Estimate yours</h2><p>Enter your gross income and effective tax rate into the take-home pay calculator for a quick, realistic net figure.</p>",
